{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently predicts a smooth, monotonic trend (either flat or slightly upward) while true outcomes exhibit high-amplitude spikes (e.g., rising to 80+) followed by sharp crashes. Apply a volatility multiplier to forecast ranges rather than point estimates.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after sentiment spikes: When sentiment history shows consecutive high values (1.00) followed by a price spike, the model fails to anticipate the subsequent rapid decline. Reduce forecasts by 15-20% immediately following any peak in the true price series, regardless of sentiment persistence.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend reversal: The model exhibits a significant lag in recognizing trend changes, often continuing to forecast upward movement for 5-10 days after the true price has peaked and begun falling. Shift the forecast trajectory downward by 3-5 days relative to the observed turning points in the history.",
    "Invert sentiment correlation for short-term horizons: High sentiment scores (1.00) in the history often precede price drops in the immediate 3-7 day window, contrary to the model's assumption of positive correlation. Apply a negative sentiment adjustment to the first 7 days of the forecast when recent sentiment is high.",
    "Cap long-term forecasts at historical mean: The model projects a slow, linear increase to ~70+ over 30 days, ignoring the mean-reverting nature of the market observed in the true data (which oscillates between 63 and 80). Cap the 30-day forecast at the 18-day rolling mean and flatten the slope."
  ]
}
